**Current Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) environment supporting multiple users, role hierarchies, temporary role delegation, revocation, and concurrent request processing. The system enforces authorization correctness, maintains delegation and permission consistency, and ensures safety and liveness under concurrency. Users have clearance levels (High, Medium, Low), and objects have classification levels (High, Medium, Low). Requests are classified by the highest classification level of affected objects and must comply with user clearance. Higher-clearance users can approve or reject lower-clearance user requests. Delegation and revocation of roles are governed by clearance and administrative rules. The system includes an Emergency Module to manage exceptional scenarios, which alters standard behaviors under specific triggers and constraints. Emergency mode is abstracted as a fixed duration but may terminate early if both administrators who activated it jointly deactivate it. The system must always eventually return to Normal mode after Emergency mode. Assigned roles are permanent (never expire or get revoked), whereas delegated roles may expire or be revoked.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- The set of administrators can range from 0 to 4, depending on scenario. Administrator uniqueness is based on set semantics; duplicate entries are not possible.
- Emergency mode can only be triggered from Normal mode and requires exactly two distinct administrators, acting jointly (no more, no fewer). If fewer or more than two administrators participate, Emergency mode cannot be entered.
- Emergency mode is time-bounded, abstracted as a fixed duration; it may terminate early if both participating administrators jointly deactivate Emergency. The system must eventually return to Normal mode after Emergency mode.
- FIFO ordering for request processing can be bypassed only during Emergency mode, and only for high-classification requests.
- Mutual exclusivity of roles is suspended for all users only during Emergency mode. During Emergency mode, any user may simultaneously hold any combination of roles, including roles that are mutually exclusive in Normal mode.
- Emergency privilege is granted automatically and only to the two administrators who triggered Emergency mode (emAdmins), only during Emergency mode, and is revoked explicitly and immediately at the first Normal state after Emergency mode ends. EmergencyPrivilege must never be granted outside Emergency mode or persist beyond the first Normal state after Emergency ends.
- Roles delegated during Emergency mode must expire exactly at the first Normal state after Emergency mode ends; any delegation assigned during Emergency mode must expire at that state, overriding any user-specified expiry time.
- Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode are not automatically revoked, but may still expire or be revoked by an administrator during Emergency mode.
- Only the administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger (the two emAdmins) are required to update their credentials after Emergency mode ends.
- No role is mutually exclusive with itself, and mutual exclusivity is symmetric.
- For all business scenarios, the set of administrators is bounded as above, but the system may contain a larger number of users for coverage and verification purposes.
- Self-delegation is prohibited; a user cannot delegate a role to itself. This is an explicit system invariant.
- Assigned roles are permanent: they never expire and cannot be revoked. Delegated roles may expire or be revoked.
- A user can only have one clearance level at any time.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Trigger  
Exactly two distinct administrators, while the system is in Normal mode, are required to jointly trigger entry into Emergency mode. No more and no fewer than two administrators may jointly trigger Emergency mode. Only these two participating administrators (emAdmins) are considered for all subsequent emergency-related requirements. Emergency mode cannot be entered unless exactly two administrators trigger it.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
Emergency mode is active only for a limited (bounded) time, abstracted as a fixed duration; however, it may terminate early if both participating administrators (emAdmins) jointly deactivate Emergency. The system must always eventually return to Normal mode after Emergency mode.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
R3.1. During Emergency mode, request processing must occur by classification: all high-classification requests are processed first (possibly concurrently), then all medium-classification requests are processed concurrently, and finally all low-classification requests are processed.  
R3.2. During Emergency mode, mutual exclusivity of roles is suspended for all users. It must be possible and observable for any user to simultaneously hold any combination of roles, including at least one pair of roles that are mutually exclusive in Normal mode. Assignment and delegation may both contribute to such combinations.  
R3.3. During Emergency mode, only the two administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger (emAdmins) temporarily hold emergency privileges, and EmergencyPrivilege is granted automatically and only to them for the duration of Emergency mode. EmergencyPrivilege must never be granted outside Emergency mode and must always be revoked explicitly and immediately at the first Normal state after Emergency mode ends. The revocation time for each EmergencyPrivilege must be explicitly tracked.

R4: Post-Emergency Restoration  
R4.1. At the first Normal state after Emergency mode ends, EmergencyPrivilege must always be revoked from the two administrators (emAdmins) who participated in the Emergency trigger, with explicit record of the revocation time. EmergencyPrivilege must not persist beyond that state, including the first step after Emergency mode ends.  
R4.2. After Emergency mode ends, any roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked; specifically:  
  - R4.2a. Any delegation assigned during Emergency mode must expire exactly at the first Normal state after Emergency ends, without exception, overriding any user-specified expiry time.  
  - R4.2b. Delegated roles from before Emergency mode are not automatically revoked solely due to the transition out of Emergency mode; they must remain active until their explicit expiry or explicit revocation, including through and after Emergency mode, unless explicitly revoked.  
R4.3. After Emergency mode ends, only the two administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger are required to update their credentials.

R5: Delegation Clearance, Tracking, and Restrictions  
R5.1. Users with higher clearance may temporarily delegate one or more of their assigned roles to a lower-clearance user, provided that all the following conditions hold at the time of delegation:  
  - The delegator holds the role by direct assignment (not by delegation), and retains direct assignment throughout the delegation period.  
  - The delegatee’s clearance is less than or equal to both the role’s minimum clearance and the delegator’s clearance.  
  - The delegatee is not the same as the delegator (self-delegation prohibited).  
  - Delegation chains are forbidden: a role obtained via delegation may not be further delegated under any circumstance.  
  - Delegations must not be permitted where the delegator’s eligibility is due only to a prior delegation.
R5.2. Delegation can only occur if the delegator holds the role by direct assignment at the time of delegation, and throughout the delegation period; assigned roles never expire or get revoked.
R5.3. Self-delegation is prohibited; a user cannot delegate a role to itself. This prohibition is enforced as a system invariant.
R5.4. Every delegation must include an explicit expiry time, and remains active until its expiry time unless revoked earlier. No delegation expires before it is granted, and delegatedAt must be strictly less than expiresAt (zero-duration delegations are not allowed).
R5.5. All delegations and privilege requests (except for automatic EmergencyPrivilege grants) must be tracked with the identity of the approver or denier.
R5.6. All privilege and delegation requests (except automatically granted EmergencyPrivilege) must be tracked with the identity of the user who approved or denied the request, and the decision made.
R5.7. Every request (privilege or delegation) must eventually be processed (either approved or rejected); no request may remain indefinitely pending.
R5.8. There must be a record of assigned roles for each user so that delegation eligibility (direct assignment) and retention throughout the delegation’s duration can be verified.

R6: Mutual Exclusivity Enforcement  
R6.1. In Normal mode, no user may simultaneously hold two mutually exclusive roles by any means (assignment or delegation). Mutual exclusivity applies globally across all roles held (direct or delegated) in Normal mode, except during Emergency mode.
R6.2. In Emergency mode, mutual exclusivity of roles is suspended for all users, and any user may simultaneously hold any combination of roles, including mutually exclusive pairs, whether via assignment or delegation.

R7: Prohibition of Delegation Chains  
R7.1. A role obtained via delegation may not be further delegated under any circumstance.

NEWLY DISCOVERED REQUIREMENT GAPS
==================================
- For verification coverage only (not business requirements), enforce the following:  
    - Emergency mode must always be exercised in verification scenarios to validate Emergency-specific requirements.  
    - The system should include users with each clearance level (High, Medium, Low) for coverage, but this is not required in all business scenarios; it is enforced only for verification.  
    - At least one pair of mutually exclusive roles must exist at all times; one pair is sufficient for coverage.  
    - During Emergency mode, at least one user must simultaneously hold both roles of a mutually exclusive pair, and this may be achieved via assignment and/or delegation, not limited to delegation only. It must not be possible for any user to hold both roles of a mutually exclusive pair in Normal mode (by assignment or delegation).  
    - At least one delegation must be granted before Emergency mode with an explicit expiry after Emergency mode ends, to verify persistence through mode transitions.  
    - For verification, the User, Role, Object, and Request scopes must each be set to at least 5 to ensure full coverage.  
    - For each positive test scenario, scenario preconditions (such as Emergency present, required delegations present, role exclusivity present) must always be satisfied in verification runs.  
    - For verification, negative scenarios (such as self-delegation, delegation chain, or delegator not assigned the role) are enforced via global facts and assertions; explicit negative scenario construction is not required if facts/assertions are sufficient.  
    - All requirements and coverage constraints must be reflected in the All_Requirements predicate; do not create duplicate predicates with different names. Incremental run statements should be kept for incremental analysis.

PROVISIONAL ASSUMPTIONS FOR VERIFICATION
========================================
- For Alloy model checking, all coverage facts are enforced globally, not scenario-locally: (a) Emergency mode is always exercised in verification, (b) at least one pair of mutually exclusive roles exists, (c) at least one user is assigned two mutually exclusive roles during Emergency mode (via assignment and/or delegation), and (d) at least one delegation spans Emergency mode (granted before, expires after).
- For verification, include users with each clearance level (High, Medium, Low) where required for coverage, but this is not enforced in all business scenarios.
- The User, Role, Object, and Request scopes should each be set to at least 5 for verification to ensure all coverage conditions are met, regardless of the fixed administrator count.
- For verification scenario naming, include `positive` or `negative` to clarify expected outcome and scenario coverage.
- No particular constraints are required on which roles are mutually exclusive for verification; mutual exclusion is abstract and not role-specific.
- Clearance level comparisons in Alloy must use appropriate techniques due to non-integer ordering; delegation clearance checks must use "less than or equal to" for both the role’s minimum clearance and the delegator’s clearance.
- Do not create redundant assertions that simply restate enforced facts; avoid vacuous assertions.
